  writing: [
    {
      type:"Task 1", min:150, time:"20 phút",
      prompt:"The diagram shows how drinking water is produced from river water at a water treatment plant. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features.",
      charts:[{type:"process",title:"Producing drinking water from a river",steps:["Water is pumped from the river","Large objects are removed by a screen","Chemicals make small particles stick together","Particles sink to the bottom of a tank","Water passes through sand filters","Chlorine is added to kill bacteria","Clean water is stored and sent to homes"]}],
      plan:["Mở bài: paraphrase (The diagram illustrates…)","Overview: 7 giai đoạn, từ bơm nước sông đến cấp nước sạch cho nhà","Thân 1: giai đoạn 1–4 (bơm, lọc rác, hoá chất, lắng)","Thân 2: giai đoạn 5–7 (lọc cát, chlorine, chứa và phân phối)","Dùng bị động + first, next, after that, finally"],
      model:"The diagram illustrates the process by which river water is turned into drinking water at a treatment plant.\n\nOverall, there are seven stages in the process, beginning with water being taken from a river and ending with clean water being delivered to homes. The process involves removing dirt in several steps and then killing bacteria.\n\nFirst, water is pumped from the river into the plant. It then passes through a screen, which removes large objects such as leaves, plastic and branches. Next, chemicals are added to the water. These cause very small particles of dirt to stick together, so that they become heavier. After that, the water is left in a large tank, where the particles sink to the bottom.\n\nIn the following stage, the water flows through filters made of sand, which remove any remaining particles. Chlorine is then added in order to kill bacteria and make the water safe to drink. Finally, the clean water is stored in a reservoir before being sent through pipes to people's homes.\n\nIn summary, the process combines physical and chemical methods to produce safe drinking water."
    },
    {
      type:"Task 2", min:250, time:"40 phút",
      prompt:"In many countries, people waste a lot of water. Why does this happen, and what can be done to encourage people to use less water?",
      plan:["Mở bài: paraphrase + nêu sẽ bàn nguyên nhân và giải pháp","Thân 1: nguyên nhân (nước rẻ, không biết khan hiếm, thói quen, đường ống rò rỉ)","Thân 2: giải pháp (giá theo bậc, giáo dục, thiết bị tiết kiệm, sửa ống)","Kết bài: tóm tắt"],
      model:"Although clean water is becoming scarce in many parts of the world, a large amount of it is still wasted every day. This essay will discuss why this happens and suggest some ways to encourage people to use water more carefully.\n\nThere are several reasons why people waste water. The main one is that water is often very cheap. When people pay only a small amount each month, they have little reason to think about how much they use. Secondly, many people simply do not realise that water is limited, especially if they live in a city where clean water always comes out of the tap. Bad habits are another cause. For example, some people leave the tap running while they brush their teeth or take very long showers. Finally, in some countries, old and broken pipes lose a huge amount of water before it even reaches homes.\n\nA number of measures could help. Governments could introduce a pricing system in which households that use more water pay a higher rate per litre. This would encourage people to save without making basic water unaffordable. Education is also important: schools and the media could run campaigns to explain why water should be saved. In addition, people could be given discounts on water-saving devices, such as showers and toilets that use less water. Local authorities should also repair leaking pipes.\n\nIn conclusion, water is wasted mainly because it is cheap and people are unaware of the problem. Better pricing, education and technology could greatly reduce this waste."
    }
  ],
  speaking: [
    {
      p1:["Do you drink a lot of water every day?","Do you prefer swimming in the sea or in a pool?","Is there a river or lake near where you live?","Do you enjoy rainy days?"],
      p1sample:{q:"Do you enjoy rainy days?",a:"It depends. If I'm at home, I love rainy days, because I can drink tea and read a book. But if I have to ride my motorbike to work, I really hate them, because the streets flood very easily in my city."},
      cue:{t:"Describe a time when you had a problem with water.",pts:["when and where it happened","what the problem was","what you did"],end:"and explain how you felt about it."},
      p2sample:"I'd like to talk about a time when our apartment had no running water for three days. It happened last summer in Hanoi, during a very hot week. One morning I turned on the tap and nothing came out. Later we found out that a main pipe in our area had broken. At first I thought it would be fixed in a few hours, but it took three days. We had to buy large bottles of water from the shop, and my neighbours and I carried buckets from a water truck that the city sent. It was exhausting, especially because it was over thirty-eight degrees. I felt frustrated at first, but it also made me realise how lucky we normally are, and since then I've tried hard not to waste water.",
      p3:["Why do some countries have problems getting clean water?","Who should pay for clean water: individuals or the government?","How can schools teach children to save water?"]
    },
    {
      p1:["Can you swim?","Do you like water sports?","How often do you go to the beach?","What do you usually do near water when you have free time?"],
      p1sample:{q:"Can you swim?",a:"Yes, but not very well. I learned when I was about ten, at a public pool near my school. I can swim breaststroke, but I've never been brave enough to swim far out in the sea."},
      cue:{t:"Describe a water sport or activity you would like to try.",pts:["what the activity is","where you would do it","who you would do it with"],end:"and explain why you would like to try it."},
      p2sample:"I'd really like to try scuba diving. I think the best place to do it in Vietnam would be Nha Trang or Con Dao, because the water there is clear and there are lots of coral reefs. I'd like to go with my cousin, who is a qualified diver, so I would feel safer. I'd probably need to take a short course first, to learn how to use the equipment and how to breathe under water. The main reason I want to try it is that I've always been fascinated by the ocean. I've watched lots of documentaries about marine life, and I'd love to see colourful fish and turtles with my own eyes rather than on a screen.",
      p3:["Why are water sports becoming more popular?","Should all children learn to swim at school?","How does pollution affect the sea and beaches in your country?"]
    }
  ]
